WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.540
Blayne Weaver: Not that it had been easy to
raise money because I had only directed one

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:05.770
feature film before and that feature had not
made its money back, so I didn’t have much

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:07.240
of a track record.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:13.250
Film Courage: How has experience impacted
your financing movies?

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:17.300
Blayne Weaver: Well…that’s a good question.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:21.050
I think every movie is different when it comes
to financing, you know?

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:27.279
Some movies lend themselves more to…like
[my movie] WEATHER GIRL was very user-friendly.

00:00:27.279 --> 00:00:29.839
It was a romantic comedy.

00:00:29.839 --> 00:00:31.099
It was easy.

00:00:31.099 --> 00:00:36.860
It didn’t offend anybody…not that it was
easy to raise money because I had only directed

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:39.510
one feature film before and that feature had
not made its money back.

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:42.170
So I did not have much of a track record.

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:47.730
But Jayne Lynch was a friend of Tricia O’Kelley’s
and she had agreed to star in it.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:50.580
Once Jane Lynch is in something, everybody
wants to be in it.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:55.410
We had a really great cast built before we
had any money, you know?

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:58.620
Which is rare and I’ve never seen that happen
in anybody’s movie.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:05.519
I think Jane Lynch being the “Lynch Pin”
(smiles) of that movie was very specific to

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:06.920
how that movie got financed.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:13.400
With 6 MONTH RULE, we found an investor that
believed in me.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.049
Me and my business partner went to the same
high school that we went to.

00:01:17.049 --> 00:01:19.969
And now he is an incredibly successful business
man.

00:01:19.969 --> 00:01:24.909
Like I was pitching 6 MONTH RULE all over
the place and I don’t even know if he read

00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:25.909
the script.

00:01:25.909 --> 00:01:29.450
He believed in what we were doing and was
interested in movies.

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:30.899
So he financed that.

00:01:30.899 --> 00:01:39.450
And then CUT TO THE CHASE was this great amalgam
of $5,000 here…$5,000 here…until we raised

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:42.170
over 100 grand.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:46.180
All people who were just betting on us.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:48.340
And that was a really personal thing.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:55.630
Like people just wanted a Shreveport movie
from a Shreveport boy and they got it!

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:56.630
Every one (film) is different, though.

00:01:56.630 --> 00:02:00.719
But the only consistent is that you have to
take every meeting.

00:02:00.719 --> 00:02:04.460
You have to talk to everybody because you
never know where that money is going to come

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:06.689
from and it’s always a surprise.

00:02:06.689 --> 00:02:13.069
Like we had an experience where we had a driver
for a movie that I was on and he drove me

00:02:13.069 --> 00:02:14.700
to set everyday.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:15.870
Very cool guy.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:16.870
Liked me very much.

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:20.480
At the end of the movie he was like “I have
a friend who has a connection to money.”

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:22.569
And I was like “You should talk to my business
partner.”

00:02:22.569 --> 00:02:27.980
And we ended up that Secret Identity [his
film company] financed the movie with the

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:29.350
friend of the driver.

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:31.190
Like…who would have thought?

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:33.409
But it comes from all over the place.

00:02:33.409 --> 00:02:35.900
You’ve got to always be ready.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:40.959
Sometimes you have to ask the awkward question
“Will you give me money?”

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:41.959
[laughs].

00:02:41.959 --> 00:02:44.610
Film Courage: And so with crowdfunding, it
sounds like it’s good for maybe part of

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:47.830
it [the film finance] but especially in this
landscape it sounds like it might be tougher

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:48.830
now?

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:53.780
Blayne Weaver: I think it would be tough…I
mean we raised 20 grand ($20,000) and it was

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:55.400
hard work.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:57.180
Every single day you have to be out there.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:02.270
And you’ve got to be hitting people over
the head with it all the time.

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:05.370
[Filmmaker] Paul Osborne always says that
I don’t hit hard enough.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:06.370
But I do!

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:09.510
I hit really hard and I was able to raise
$20,000.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.260
But it was by hook or by crook.

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:16.620
I pulled that money from a ton of really great
people.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:24.239
But I don’t know how much more you can get
without some kind of hook, you know what I

00:03:24.239 --> 00:03:25.239
mean?

00:03:25.239 --> 00:03:28.379
The perfect sweater or whatever those things
that make a million dollars [on crowdfunding

00:03:28.379 --> 00:03:29.379
platforms].

00:03:29.379 --> 00:03:31.550
I don’t know how they do it but I would
love to get in on that.

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:32.510
Question for the Viewers: How many investors
put money into your last movie?

